  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe central idea is to enforce reflection symmetry about x=1, which reduces the degrees of freedom by half, speeding up optimization and leveraging problem structure. The parent solution's objective may not strictly satisfy symmetry, which is believed to be an optimal property of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. By formulating the optimization over a smaller variable space and incorporating symmetry constraints, we aim to escape local optima and achieve stronger lower bounds. The approach is structured as a multi-stage optimization with periodic equipment maintenance to ensure sustained search: Stage 1 adapts hyperparameters for each resolution level; Stage 2 reduces dimensionality via symmetry; Stage 3 applies dimension-restricted optimization followed by projection post-refinement; Stage 4 coordinates strategies across resolutions. The primary bottleneck addressed is the local optimum trapping in the parent program, mitigated by grayscale decoding and branch-and-bound integration in adversarial conditions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n- Variables: Haptic function represented on a grid of n_points, with optimal candidates satisfying reflection symmetry: h(x) = 1 - h(2 - x) for x in [0,2]\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b_0^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, calculated via np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- Representation: Symmetrically reduced problem by operating on 0 \u2264 x \u2264 n_points/2 parameters, mapping to full vector via h[i] = h_reduced[i + j] for i < n_points/2 and h[i] = h_reduced[n_points/2 - (i - n_points/2)] for i \u2265 n_points/2\n- Objective reformulation: Incorporates symmetry-weighted Gaussian penalties to distance function at k-positions, replacing strict max with smoothed approximation to maintain convex properties in a gradient-derived subspace.\n- High-Resolution Handling: Progressive resolution scaling (400 to 1600) with expert preconditioner matrix for block-based decomposition.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n1.1 Initialization:\n- time_left = budget_s - (timerun start)\n- Define resolution sequence: [400, 800, 1600] if time > 600 for each\n- For each resolution n:\n  3.1 Generate reduced representation from initial_h_values via symmetric projection: \n      reduced_h = (initial_h_values[i] + initial_h_values[n-1-i])/2 for i in 0 to n//2-1\n      linearly interpolate or project to satisfy integral \u2248 n/2\n  3.2 Set bounds: bounds = [(0,1)]*len(reduced_h)\n  3.3 Formulate constraints:\n      - Sum(reduced_h) \u2248 n/4 (half of target, due to symmetry property)\n      - Additional soft constraints via inequality penalties: |h[i] - 0.5| penalized if out of bounds\n  3.4 Stage 1: Dimension reduction optimization (case sensitivity algorithm):\n      - Objective function: smoothed_max_overlap(h_full_vector) = np.log(\u2211exp(alpha*(conv - best_known))) / alpha\n      - Incorporate symmetry constraints via weighting matrix for j and n-1-j pairs in gradient calculation\n      - Use SLSQP with strong integer constraints enforcement (both box and equality)\n      - Transformation from reduced_re H to h_full = 2 * reduced_H - h_central adjustment for central point\n\n2.2 Stage 2: Refinement and multi-resolution coordination\n  4.1 Periodically (every 200 iterations), enforce integral via projection\n  4.2 Branch out by randomizing selection of operating resolution based expert decision:\n      Decision rule: if improvement threshold >= 0.01, select highest available resolution <= remaining time\n  4.3 For each candidate obtained in stage 1, apply local search with projected gradients\n  4.4 Maintain a separate stack for \"warm-started\" objectives at lower resolutions\n\n3.3 Stage 3: Progressive resolution expansion\n  5.1: If best score is high: upsample by doubling points at resolution boundaries\n  5.2: Apply a spectral pre-smoothing operator in the Fourier domain\n  5.3: Create a \"symmetry mask\" to enforce reflection about both x=0 and x=2\n\n3.4 Stage 4: Optimization components\n  6.1 Apply stage 1 optimization with continuousizable constraint handler\n  6.2 Compute gradient using interpolated adjoint method\n  6.3 Use an initial simplex to explore low-dimensional space\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- Initial resolution choice: Symmetry-adjusted projection of parent (400 points), followed by 800 and 1600 if time allows.\n- Gradient smoothing parameters: {alpha: 10, 50, 100} for each resolution, with stepsize reduction by 1/x\u209c at stagnation\n- Issue resolution: If multiple solutions compete, use bandpass filtering decision rule to select low-frequency candidates\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- Time allocation: Coarse: 300s, Medium: 500s, Fine: 500s\n- Memory: Must handle dense Hessian storage for block updates at higher resolutions\n- Failure mitigation: If inversion fails, borrow from LAPACK's continuousization approach; use QR factorization for regularized system\n- Check for convergence: Build info vector over iterations and use relative improvement threshold (0.01) to stop early\n- Fallback: Linearly interpolate solution from closest valid resolution if initial phase fails\n- Timer: Monitor via soft interrupt at 10 second intervals\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n- Feasibility: Projected using SLSQP with integral adjustment upon completion\n- Final score: median of 3 independent compute_c5 evaluations\n- Require explicit return: (best_h, best_score, n) \n- Check: post-processing ensures |h[i]-0.5| <= 0.5 and integral is tolerant within 1e-6\n- Fast validation by outsourced compute_c5 using parent's correlate method",
